36:46595–46623.

</span></li>
    
</ul>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Additional Figures</h2>

<figure id="A1.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15317v1/score_distribution_before_and_after_fusion.png" id="A1.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/226;" width="452" height="226" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Score distributions from individual critics and after fusion. The fusion results in a more balanced distribution.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15317v1/weight_change.png" id="A1.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/198;" width="452" height="198" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Critic weights before (raw) and after fusion across seven data sources. The fused weights adaptively adjust each critic’s influence per domain.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Derivation and Analysis of Multi-Expert Fusion Method</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Notation and Problem Setup</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this appendix, we provide a detailed derivation and theoretical justification for the multi-expert fusion method presented in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 3.2 VERITAS: A Four-Stage Pipeline for Data Critique and Refinement ‣ 3 Method ‣ VERITAS: Leveraging Vision Priors and Expert Fusion to Improve Multimodal Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> of the main paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each data source (domain) <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">- Let <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{d}(n)=(s_{1,d}(n),s_{2,d}(n),s_{3,d}(n))^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{d}(n)=(s_{1,d}(n),s_{2,d}(n),s_{3,d}(n))^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> be the vector of raw scores assigned by the three expert critics for sample <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in domain <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">- Let <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{d}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{d}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> be the latent true score (e.g., a human-annotated score) for sample <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in domain <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, assumed to have finite variance <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to construct an estimator <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{d}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{d}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> of the true score <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{d}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{d}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> by linearly combining the experts’ scores:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{d}(n)=\mathbf{w}_{d}^{\top}\mathbf{z}_{d}(n)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{d}(n)=\mathbf{w}_{d}^{\top}\mathbf{z}_{d}(n),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{d}=(w_{1,d},w_{2,d},w_{3,d})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{d}=(w_{1,d},w_{2,d},w_{3,d})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> are the weights for domain <math id="A2.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{d}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{d}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> are the normalized scores, as defined below.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We aim to find weights <math id="A2.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> that minimize the expected squared error (risk):</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\hat{y}_{d}(n)-y_{d}(n)\right)^{2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\hat{y}_{d}(n)-y_{d}(n)\right)^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Domain-Wise Z-Normalization</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure comparability across different experts and domains, we perform z-score normalization of the raw scores within each domain:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{m,d}(n)=\frac{s_{m,d}(n)-\mu_{m,d}}{\sigma_{m,d}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{m,d}(n)=\frac{s_{m,d}(n)-\mu_{m,d}}{\sigma_{m,d}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{m,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{m,d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{m,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{m,d}</annotation></semantics></math> are the mean and standard deviation of expert <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>’s scores in domain <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By this normalization, the standardized scores <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{m,d}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{m,d}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[z_{m,d}]=0,\quad\text{Var}[z_{m,d}]=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[z_{m,d}]=0,\quad\text{Var}[z_{m,d}]=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Any linear combination of these normalized scores will have expectation <math id="A2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> if the weights sum to zero. However, since we aim to produce a meaningful aggregate score, we instead constrain the weights to sum to one:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{m=1}^{3}w_{m,d}=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{m=1}^{3}w_{m,d}=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Although this introduces bias in the estimator, we correct for it later through the percentile rescaling step.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) Based Raw Weights</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming that each expert’s score is an unbiased estimator of the true score corrupted by noise, we model:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{m,d}(n)=y_{d}(n)+\eta_{m,d}(n)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{m,d}(n)=y_{d}(n)+\eta_{m,d}(n),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{m,d}(n)\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{m,d}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{m,d}(n)\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{m,d}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the noise in expert <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>’s score within domain <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The expected risk (mean squared error) of our estimator is then:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\hat{y}_{d}(n)-y_{d}(n)\right)^{2}\right]=\sum_{m=1}^{3}w_{m,d}^{2}\sigma_{m,d}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\hat{y}_{d}(n)-y_{d}(n)\right)^{2}\right]=\sum_{m=1}^{3}w_{m,d}^{2}\sigma_{m,d}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">since the normalized scores <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{m,d}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{m,d}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> are centered with unit variance.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To minimize <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, we set the weights proportional to the inverse of the variances:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{m,d}\propto\frac{1}{\sigma_{m,d}^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{m,d}\propto\frac{1}{\sigma_{m,d}^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, we estimate <math id="A2.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{m,d}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{m,d}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> using the variance of the residuals (noise) within domain <math id="A2.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{m,d}^{2}\approx\text{Var}\left(s_{m,d}(n)-\bar{s}_{d}(n)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{m,d}^{2}\approx\text{Var}\left(s_{m,d}(n)-\bar{s}_{d}(n)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{s}_{d}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{s}_{d}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is the mean score for sample <math id="A2.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> across all experts in domain <math id="A2.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, the raw weights are computed based on the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR):</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{raw\_}w_{m,d}=\frac{\sigma_{m,d}}{\text{noise}_{m,d}+\epsilon}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>raw_</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mtext>noise</mtext><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{raw\_}w_{m,d}=\frac{\sigma_{m,d}}{\text{noise}_{m,d}+\epsilon},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{noise}_{m,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>noise</mtext><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{noise}_{m,d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard deviation of the residuals <math id="A2.SS3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{m,d}(n)=s_{m,d}(n)-\bar{s}_{d}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{m,d}(n)=s_{m,d}(n)-\bar{s}_{d}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a small constant to prevent division by zero.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>James–Stein Shrinkage Estimator</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The raw weights computed above may still have high variance, especially in domains with a small number of samples (<math id="A2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> small). To address this, we apply James–Stein shrinkage, which shrinks the domain-specific weights toward the global mean weights, balancing bias and variance.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We compute the global mean weights:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{w}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{d\in\mathcal{D}}\mathbf{w}_{d}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{w}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{d\in\mathcal{D}}\mathbf{w}_{d},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">and then apply shrinkage:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{d}=\alpha_{d}\mathbf{w}_{d}+(1-\alpha_{d})\bar{\mathbf{w}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{d}=\alpha_{d}\mathbf{w}_{d}+(1-\alpha_{d})\bar{\mathbf{w}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where the shrinkage factor is:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{d}=\frac{N_{d}}{N_{d}+\lambda}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{d}=\frac{N_{d}}{N_{d}+\lambda},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">with <math id="A2.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> being a hyperparameter set to <math id="A2.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=100</annotation></semantics></math> in our implementation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">This results in the adjusted weights <math id="A2.SS4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are a convex combination of the domain-specific weights <math id="A2.SS4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and the global mean weights <math id="A2.SS4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The choice of <math id="A2.SS4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that in domains with large <math id="A2.SS4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we trust the domain-specific weights more, while in domains with small <math id="A2.SS4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we rely more on the global weights.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Risk Reduction Proof</h4>

<div id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Substituting the shrinkage weights into the risk <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, we find that the expected risk under the shrinkage estimator is less than or equal to that under the raw weights:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A2.E23X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E23X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta R_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta R_{d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E23X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=R(\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{d})-R(\mathbf{w}_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=R(\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{d})-R(\mathbf{w}_{d})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E23Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E23Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\frac{(1-\alpha_{d})^{2}}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{m=1}^{3}(w_{m,d}-\bar{w}_{m})^{2}\leq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\frac{(1-\alpha_{d})^{2}}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{m=1}^{3}(w_{m,d}-\bar{w}_{m})^{2}\leq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">since <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\alpha_{d})^{2}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\alpha_{d})^{2}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and the squared differences are non-negative. Equality holds only when <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{d}=\bar{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{d}=\bar{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the James–Stein shrinkage estimator does not increase the risk and typically reduces it.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.5 </span>Percentile Re-Projection (Rescaling to Target Range)</h3>

<div id="A2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After fusing the normalized scores using the adjusted weights, we obtain the estimated scores:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}(n)=\sum_{m=1}^{3}\hat{w}_{m,d}\,z_{m,d}(n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></munderover><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}(n)=\sum_{m=1}^{3}\hat{w}_{m,d}\,z_{m,d}(n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, the distribution of <math id="A2.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> may not be standard normal due to the weighting and shrinkage. To map the fused scores back to the original scoring range [0, 5], we apply a percentile-based linear rescaling.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We compute the lower and upper quantiles (e.g., 5% and 95%) of the fused scores <math id="A2.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> across all samples, denoted as <math id="A2.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\text{low}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>low</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\text{low}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\text{high}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>high</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\text{high}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The final estimated scores are then:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{S}(n)=5\times\text{clip}\left(\frac{\hat{z}(n)-q_{\text{low}}}{q_{\text{high}}-q_{\text{low}}},\,0,1\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>clip</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>low</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>high</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>low</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn> 0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{S}(n)=5\times\text{clip}\left(\frac{\hat{z}(n)-q_{\text{low}}}{q_{\text{high}}-q_{\text{low}}},\,0,1\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{clip}(x,0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>clip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{clip}(x,0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> constrains <math id="A2.SS5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to the interval [0, 1].</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">This rescaling ensures that the fused scores <math id="A2.SS5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{S}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{S}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> lie within the desired range [0, 5], maintains the ordering (monotonicity), and reduces the impact of outliers by capping the extreme values.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Automated Error Injection in CLEVR Dataset to Create Answer Quality Tiers</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate our evaluator’s performance across varying answer qualities in an out-of-domain (OOD) setting, we modified the CLEVR dataset by introducing errors to create three tiers of answer quality:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High-quality answers (Tier H):</span> Original correct answers were kept unchanged.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Medium-quality answers (Tier M):</span> Minor errors were introduced to make answers partially correct or slightly ambiguous. Examples include:</p>
<ul id="A3.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adjusting numerical answers by <math id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 2</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., changing "4." to "5.").</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Replacing colors with similar ones (e.g., changing "Green." to "Blue." or "Cyan.").</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Switching size attributes (e.g., changing "Large." to "Small.").</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Changing definite answers like "Yes." to uncertain responses like "Maybe." or "Cannot tell.".</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reversing material attributes (e.g., changing "Rubber." to "Metal.").</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modifying shapes to similar ones (e.g., changing "Cube." to "Sphere.").</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low-quality answers (Tier L):</span> Clear errors were introduced by replacing correct answers with incorrect values from different categories. Examples include:</p>
<ul id="A3.I1.i3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Swapping numerical answers with colors or shapes (e.g., answering "Red." instead of "3.").</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Changing "Yes." to "No." or providing contradictory statements.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Providing unrelated attributes (e.g., answering "Metal." when the question asks for a color).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Introducing nonexistent attributes (e.g., answering "Triangle." or "Plastic.", which are not present in CLEVR).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adding irrelevant explanations to incorrect answers.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This automated error injection approach allowed us to generate a test set with diverse answer qualities, facilitating a comprehensive evaluation of our model’s ability to handle varying levels of correctness in an OOD context while using LaTeX-compatible formatting for documentation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
